        "\n",
        "# Kernel Smoothing\n",
        "\n",
        "Kernel smoothing is a family of nonparametric methods for estimating smooth functions from data. Like KNN, it is based on local information. The difference is that kernel methods usually use smoothly decaying weights instead of a hard set of nearest neighbors.\n",
        "\n",
        "The main idea is:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Nearby observations should have more influence than distant observations, and the influence should decrease smoothly with distance.\n",
        "\n",
        "This chapter focuses on kernel smoothing, not kernel machines such as support vector machines or kernel ridge regression. The connection is that both use a kernel function to measure similarity, but the modeling goals are different.\n",
        "\n",
        "## KNN versus Kernel Smoothing\n",
        "\n",
        "KNN regression predicts by averaging the $K$ nearest responses:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f_{\\text{KNN}}(x_0)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{K}\n",
        "\\sum_{i\\in N_K(x_0)} y_i.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This gives equal weight to all $K$ nearest neighbors and zero weight to every other observation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Kernel smoothing instead assigns a weight to each observation based on its distance from $x_0$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_i(x_0)\n",
        "\\propto\n",
        "K\\left(\\frac{x_0-x_i}{h}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $K(\\cdot)$ is the kernel function,\n",
        "- $h>0$ is the bandwidth,\n",
        "- nearby points receive large weights,\n",
        "- distant points receive small weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Main difference\n",
        "\n",
        "KNN uses a fixed number of neighbors.\n",
        "\n",
        "Kernel smoothing uses a fixed distance scale, controlled by the bandwidth $h$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "KNN fits are often discontinuous because the neighbor set can change abruptly as $x_0$ moves. Kernel smoothers, especially with smooth kernels such as the Gaussian kernel, usually produce smooth fitted curves.\n",
        "\n",
        "The following example compares a KNN regression curve with a Gaussian kernel regression curve on the same data."
      ],
