        "## Bandwidth\n",
        "\n",
        "The bandwidth $h$ is the most important tuning parameter in kernel smoothing.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Small $h$: uses very local information and gives a flexible, variable estimate.\n",
        "- Large $h$: averages over a wider region and gives a smoother, more biased estimate.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the same bias-variance tradeoff seen in KNN.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Bandwidth and smoothness\n",
        "\n",
        "Smaller bandwidth means less smoothing.\n",
        "\n",
        "Larger bandwidth means more smoothing.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Kernel Density Estimation\n",
        "\n",
        "Kernel density estimation estimates the density of a random variable from observed data\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_1,\\ldots,x_n.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The kernel density estimator is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{n}\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "K_h(x,x_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{nh}\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "K\\left(\\frac{x-x_i}{h}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The interpretation is simple: place a small smooth bump at every observation, then add the bumps together and divide by $n$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Uniform Kernel and Histograms\n",
        "\n",
        "A histogram counts observations inside bins. A uniform-kernel density estimate counts observations in a moving window:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "\\#\\{x_i:x_i\\in[x-h/2,x+h/2]\\}\n",
        "}{nh}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is like a histogram whose bin center moves continuously with $x$."
