      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The small bandwidth produces many local bumps. The large bandwidth hides local structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Expectation of the Kernel Density Estimator\n",
        "\n",
        "Assume $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ are independent samples from a density $f$. The estimator is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{n}\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\frac{1}{h}\n",
        "K\\left(\\frac{x-x_i}{h}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Its expectation is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "\\Exp[\\hat f(x)]\n",
        "&=\n",
        "\\Exp\\left[\n",
        "\\frac{1}{h}K\\left(\\frac{x-X}{h}\\right)\n",
        "\\right] \\\\\n",
        "&=\n",
        "\\int\n",
        "\\frac{1}{h}\n",
        "K\\left(\\frac{x-t}{h}\\right)\n",
        "f(t)\\,dt.\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u=\\frac{x-t}{h},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "t=x-hu.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Exp[\\hat f(x)]\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\int\n",
        "K(u)f(x-hu)\\,du.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $h$ is small and $f$ is smooth, then $f(x-hu)$ is close to $f(x)$. Since $\\int K(u)\\,du=1$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Exp[\\hat f(x)]\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "f(x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This explains why kernel density estimation can recover the true density when the bandwidth is small enough, but not too small.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Kernel Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "Kernel regression estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f(x)=\\Exp[Y\\mid X=x].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The most common kernel regression estimator is the Nadaraya-Watson estimator.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#def-nadaraya-watson}\n",
        "## Nadaraya-Watson estimator\n",
        "\n",
        "Given training data $(x_i,y_i)$, the Nadaraya-Watson estimator is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n K_h(x,x_i)y_i\n",
        "}{\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n K_h(x,x_i)\n",
        "}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a weighted average of the responses. The weights are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_i(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "K_h(x,x_i)\n",
        "}{\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^n K_h(x,x_j)\n",
        "}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n w_i(x)y_i,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n w_i(x)=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### Gaussian Kernel Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "For the Gaussian kernel,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "K_h(x,x_i)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{h\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\n",
        "\\exp\\left\\{\n",
        "-\\frac{(x-x_i)^2}{2h^2}\n",
        "\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Because the Nadaraya-Watson estimator normalizes the weights, the constant $1/(h\\sqrt{2\\pi})$ cancels out. For regression weights, it is enough to use\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\exp\\left\\{\n",
        "-\\frac{(x-x_i)^2}{2h^2}\n",
        "\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$"
      ],
