      "source": [
        "## Kernel Smoothing in Multiple Dimensions\n",
        "\n",
        "For $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^p$, a common Gaussian kernel is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "K_h(x,x_i)\n",
        "\\propto\n",
        "\\exp\\left(\n",
        "-\\frac{\\|x-x_i\\|^2}{2h^2}\n",
        "\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is similar to KNN in that it relies on distances in feature space. Therefore, scaling matters.\n",
        "\n",
        "In multiple dimensions, kernel smoothing faces the curse of dimensionality. As $p$ increases, local neighborhoods become sparse. To get enough nearby points, the bandwidth must grow, which increases bias.\n",
        "\n",
        "For $p$ dimensions, bandwidth rates are often of order\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "n^{-1/(p+4)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This gets slower as $p$ increases, which is another way to see the curse of dimensionality.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-caution}\n",
        "## Bandwidth conventions\n",
        "\n",
        "Different software packages may define bandwidth differently. Some use a raw scale $h$ inside the kernel. Others use a span, a nearest-neighbor fraction, or an internally rescaled bandwidth. Always check the documentation before comparing bandwidth values across implementations.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Relation to KNN\n",
        "\n",
        "Both KNN and kernel smoothing are weighted-average methods.\n",
        "\n",
        "KNN can be written as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat f(x_0)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n w_i(x_0)y_i,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_i(x_0)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "1/K, & i\\in N_K(x_0),\\\\\n",
        "0, & i\\notin N_K(x_0).\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Kernel regression uses\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_i(x_0)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "K_h(x_0,x_i)\n",
        "}{\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^n K_h(x_0,x_j)\n",
        "}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The difference is the shape of the weights:\n",
        "\n",
        "- KNN uses a hard cutoff based on rank.\n",
        "- Kernel regression uses smooth weights based on distance.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "Kernel smoothing:\n",
        "\n",
        "- estimates densities or regression functions using local weighted averages,\n",
        "- uses a kernel function to assign distance-based weights,\n",
        "- is controlled mainly by the bandwidth $h$,\n",
        "- produces smoother estimates than KNN when the kernel is smooth,\n",
        "- has the same bias-variance tradeoff as other nonparametric methods,\n",
        "- can suffer from boundary bias,\n",
        "- becomes harder in high-dimensional feature spaces.\n",
        "\n",
        "The practical workflow is:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Scale features if distances are based on multiple variables.\n",
        "2. Choose a kernel, often Gaussian.\n",
        "3. Tune the bandwidth by validation or cross-validation.\n",
        "4. Check whether the fitted curve is undersmoothed or oversmoothed."
      ],
      "id": "b3e69250"
